{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And it shall be for you, etc.</b> The reason for His saying <b>for you</b> is that at the beginning of the discourse HaShem said to him, ״Speak to Aaron,״ and now He comes to command Israel, as He concludes with the native and the stranger. Therefore it was necessary to say <b>for you</b>, lest one err and say that the commandment to the priests on Yom Kippur is only the first discourse that HaShem commanded at the beginning of the parashah alone, while the commandment to Israel is the affliction — as indeed is recorded in the law of HaShem, and reason too would concur with this, that the priests are exempt from affliction. Or at least the High Priest, in place of his affliction is his Divine service; and regarding Moses as well, there is reason to judge that he does not need affliction — all the more so for the one who preceded us, whose body and blood had become purified and resembled an angel of El, who has no need for affliction. Therefore HaShem commanded and said <b>and it shall be for you</b> — meaning, He is speaking with Moses and Aaron, that they too are included in the affliction. And lest you think that HaShem commands specifically Moses and Aaron and not all Israel, Scripture says <b>the native and the stranger</b>. And nearby it will be explained in another manner." "<b>An everlasting statute.</b> The reason for His saying <b>everlasting</b> is that reason might suggest that the affliction came only to atone for sin, and from now on whoever has no mark of sin in reality among Israel has no obligation of affliction; therefore He said <b>an everlasting statute.</b> And the inner meaning of the matter is: a statute of the World to Come, and it is a hidden sod." "<b>You shall afflict your souls.</b> Meaning, an affliction that encompasses the soul equally — and what is it? Withholding food. And if you say that one should afflict oneself through labor, the matter would change in two respects: first, that the portion of him that labors in that work is afflicted more, and if so there is no affliction of the soul equally, yet the Torah said ״you shall afflict your souls״; second, in such a manner it would be called affliction of the body, for the living flesh becomes weary, yet the Torah said ״your souls.״ And from now on the strength of the Karaites is emptied to naught." "<b>The native and the stranger.</b> In Torat Kohanim they said, and this is their language: ״the native״ — a native, this is a native; ״the native״ — to include the wives of the native; ״stranger״ — this is the stranger; ״the stranger״ — to include the wives of the stranger; ״among you״ — to include women and slaves — end quote. And we still need to be informed why it was necessary to mention ״native,״ and if it is for the inclusion of the letter ה to include women, that is an error, for the Almighty has words to speak in a manner that would include all those who need to be included without the addition of even a single superfluous letter." "And the matter will be explained based on what they said in tractate Sukkah (28b), and this is their language: The Master said, ״the native״ — to include women for the affliction of Yom Kippur. Rava said: It is derived from Rabbi Yehudah, as Rabbi Yehudah said in the name of Rav: Scripture equated woman to man for all punishments in the Torah. And he resolves: It was needed only for additional affliction. You might have thought to say: Since the Merciful One minimized it to additional affliction beyond punishment and warning, women should not be obligated — it teaches us — end quote. And on the basis of these words there is a reason for the verse's having needed to mention ״native,״ because the inclusion of the letter ה is for the additional affliction. Therefore HaShem was wise and mentioned both together — that which is included and the basis of that from which it is included — namely His saying <b>the native</b>, meaning, a native — this is a native who is also obligated in additional affliction as recorded elsewhere, and similar to it is that which is included by the letter ה, and there is no difference between them. And after He was compelled to say האזרח for the reason stated, He was concerned lest an errant person err and say that His saying האזרח is to exclude the stranger, who is not in the category of this commandment of Yom Kippur — therefore Scripture says <b>the stranger.</b> And if He had said גר alone without a ה, the disputant would have had proper grounds to distinguish, for the wives of the stranger would not be included, since Scripture distinguished between Israel and the stranger: regarding Israel He included the women, but regarding strangers He did not include them. Therefore it was necessary to say הגר to include the wives of the stranger. It further appears fitting to explain the reason of the verse's having needed to detail every detail, because it was necessary to detail the priests as we explained regarding the word ״for you״ — from that point it devolved upon Him to detail also the natives, lest the error arise that HaShem commands the priests alone, and once that is so, it devolved upon Him to detail all those alluded to in the parashah."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :register {:flags {3 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:52.789243141Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}